/-!
# Compact objects over the Beilinson algebra

Let `k` be a field and let `S = k[X₀, ..., Xₙ]`.  We define the algebra whose
`(i, j)` entry is the homogeneous piece `S_{j-i}` when `i ≤ j`, and is zero
otherwise.  An object of the derived category of right modules over this
algebra is compact exactly when its total cohomology is finite-dimensional over
`k`.

## Main definitions

* `StacksDraft.Perfect.beilinsonAlgebra` is the upper triangular algebra built from the
  homogeneous pieces of a polynomial ring.
* `StacksDraft.Perfect.HasFiniteTotalCohomology` says that all cohomology spaces are
  finite-dimensional and only finitely many of them are nonzero.

## Main results

* `StacksDraft.Perfect.hasFiniteTotalCohomology_iff_isCompactObject` characterizes compact
  objects of the derived category of the Beilinson algebra.

## Implementation notes

The upper triangular algebra is presented as the subalgebra generated by the
matrices having the indicated homogeneous entries.  These matrices already form
a subalgebra, so this gives the matrix algebra in the source while avoiding a
choice of bases for its homogeneous pieces.

The finite sum of dimensions in the source is expressed without choosing bases:
every cohomology module is finite-dimensional and its nonzero-degree support is
finite.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FYA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FYA)
-/

@[expose] public section

